(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:actor/wave 3), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :finalize-maintenance-execution-decision :perform-repair-directly :execute-repair-directly :complete-repair-directly :finalize-airworthiness-clearance :finalize-return-to-service-determination :override-certified-aviation-inspector-judgment :override-certified-mechanic-judgment :bypass-airworthiness-inspection :bypass-return-to-service-determination)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607121000-cloud-itonami-global-isic-isco-reverse-toposort-plan" "adr-2799007413-cloud-itonami-isco-7413-electrical-line-installers-repairers-coverage")), (:adr/status "accepted"), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "declare the engine airworthy and cleared for return to service" "declare the aircraft airworthy and cleared for return to service" "clear the engine for return to service" "clear the aircraft for return to service" "finalize the airworthiness clearance" "finalize the airworthiness determination" "finalize the return-to-service determination" "finalize the return to service determination" "finalize the repair" "finalize the maintenance-execution decision" "finalize the maintenance execution decision" "complete the repair directly" "perform the repair directly" "perform the engine repair directly" "execute the repair directly" "execute the maintenance work directly" "dispatch the mechanic to perform the repair" "sign off the airworthiness release" "sign the airworthiness release" "issue the airworthiness release" "override the certified aviation inspector's judgment" "override the certified aviation inspector" "override the certified mechanic's judgment" "override the certified aviation mechanic's judgment" "bypass the certified aviation inspector" "bypass the airworthiness inspection" "bypass the return-to-service determination")), (:adr/id 2799007232), (:actor/domain :aviation/aircraft-engine-maintenance-bay-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :no-aircraft :no-actuation :unknown-op :aircraft-mismatch :unknown-mechanic :mechanic-wrong-aircraft :scope-exclusion-violation)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "7232"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007232: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7232 Aircraft Engine Mechanics and Repairers maintenance-bay scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7232\n-- Aircraft Engine Mechanics and Repairers** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3\nbatches 1-8 already landed: 48 occupations so far).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7232\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7232\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Aircraft Engine Mechanics and\nRepairers\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and\n`:operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and\nno `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7232` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7232` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This actor carries the highest-stakes hazard framing of any Wave3\nbatch so far: aircraft engine mechanics and repairers perform\nmaintenance work where a single undetected defect can cause loss of\nlife in flight -- categorically higher-stakes than ordinary workshop\nmechanical trades**, including the electrocution/fall-from-height\nhazard already established as high-risk by `cloud-itonami-isco-7413`\n(Electrical Line Installers and Repairers). An aviation maintenance\nerror is not bounded to the worker or the immediate work site the way\nmost trade hazards are -- it can propagate to every occupant of a\nfuture flight, with no opportunity for in-flight correction. The\nclosed op allowlist therefore must NEVER include any op that directly\nfinalizes an airworthiness-clearance/return-to-service determination,\na maintenance-execution decision (performing/completing the actual\nrepair), or overrides a certified aviation inspector's/mechanic's\njudgment -- these are always a hard, permanent, structural block\n(absent from the allowlist entirely, not merely gated), never\nauto-commit-eligible under any confidence level. Any safety/anomaly\nflag on an aircraft engine ALWAYS escalates, no exceptions, ever.\n\n**This actor coordinates MAINTENANCE-BAY SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY --\nit never performs aircraft-engine maintenance work itself and never\nmakes an airworthiness-clearance decision.** It is not an authority to\ndecide when or how maintenance work happens on an aircraft engine; it\nis a coordination layer for work-record logging, crew/bay scheduling,\nsafety-concern surfacing and parts/consumables order coordination, all\ngated behind an independent governor. It never finalizes an\nairworthiness-clearance or return-to-service determination and never\nperforms or completes a repair.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7413`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7413)\n(Electrical Line Installers and Repairers: `store`/`advisor`/\n`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set,\nsame wave and an already-established high-voltage/life-safety-critical\nhazard pattern), cloned and read in full this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7232` as an OSS\ndocumentation/coordination actor for aircraft engine mechanics and\nrepairers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Aircraft Engine\nMaintenance-Bay Coordination Advisor ⊣ AeroCoordGovernor** on a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a different\nlibrary coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger. All source is portable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests\nare `clojure.test` `.clj`, matching the reference repo's own layout.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- inspection/maintenance-progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/bay/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface an engine-defect/anomaly concern,\n  ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit-eligible, no confidence-level\n  exception, ever\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- parts/consumables procurement\n  proposal, escalates above a registered cost threshold\n\nNo op in this allowlist finalizes an airworthiness-clearance or\nreturn-to-service determination, finalizes a maintenance-execution\ndecision (performing/completing the actual repair), or overrides a\ncertified aviation inspector's/mechanic's judgment -- these decision\nclasses are structurally absent from the allowlist, not merely gated\nbehind escalation.\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (`aerocoord.governor`, permanent, never\noverridable by human approval)\n\n1. **no-aircraft** -- the aircraft/engine record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action.\n2. **no-actuation** -- proposal `:effect` must be `:propose` (the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never performs\n   aircraft-engine maintenance work).\n3. **unknown-op** -- `:op` must be one of the four allowlisted\n   coordination ops; the closed op-allowlist is enforced\n   unconditionally.\n4. **aircraft-mismatch** -- if the proposal names an aircraft, it must\n   be the SAME aircraft verified for this request (defense-in-depth\n   against a proposal quietly targeting an unverified aircraft).\n5. **unknown-mechanic / mechanic-wrong-aircraft** -- if the proposal\n   references a mechanic, that mechanic must be a REGISTERED certified\n   crew member belonging to this aircraft.\n6. **scope-exclusion-violation** -- a proposal that attempts, in any\n   free-text field, to finalize an airworthiness-clearance or\n   return-to-service determination, to finalize a maintenance-\n   execution decision, or to override a certified aviation inspector's\n   or mechanic's judgment is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated\n   unconditionally on content, independent of `:op`.\n\n### Escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (`aerocoord.governor/always-escalate-ops`). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface an engine-defect/\n  anomaly/safety observation -- it never resolves the concern itself,\n  and this is unconditional: no confidence level, however high, ever\n  exempts a safety flag from human sign-off.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-order-cost-threshold` (20000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-or-below-cost-threshold`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence.\n- advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` (0.6) also escalates,\n  for any op.\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare nouns (e.g.\n\"engine\", \"airworthiness\", \"repair\") can accidentally match inside\nthe mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this risk is\nespecially sharp here because this actor's ENTIRE domain is aircraft-\nengine maintenance, so bare nouns like \"engine\"/\"airworthiness\"/\n\"repair\"/\"inspection\"/\"return to service\" are ordinary, unavoidable\nvocabulary. `aerocoord.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTION PHRASES (e.g. \"declare\nthe engine airworthy and cleared for return to service\", \"finalize\nthe repair\", \"override the certified aviation inspector's judgment\"\n-- matching the task's own worked example phrasing), never bare nouns,\nand\n`aerocoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor (including a\n`:flag-safety-concern` request whose description names an \"anomalous\nvibration reading near engine 2 turbine section, unresolved\nairworthiness question pending inspection\"), never trips the\nscope-exclusion guard. A companion test,\n`scope-exclusion-not-triggered-by-bare-domain-nouns`, asserts directly\nagainst `governor/scope-exclusion-violation?` that a rationale/\ndescription containing only bare domain nouns (\"engine 2 airworthiness\ninspection\", \"turbine repair and return to service documentation for\nthis engine overhaul\") does not trip the guard, while\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-declare-airworthy-return-to-service`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-repair`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-airworthiness-clearance`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-certified-aviation-inspector`\nand\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-certified-mechanic`\nconfirm the actual forbidden action phrases DO trip it, and\n`holds-a-scope-excluded-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`,\n`holds-a-finalize-repair-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path` and\n`holds-an-override-certified-aviation-inspector-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nconfirm the full actor graph resolves such attempts straight to\n`:hold` (a finish point, never `:request-approval`) with no record\ncommitted.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7232\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7232` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7232`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 27 tests, 69 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a fresh clone of the pushed repository (mojibake scan: 0\n  replacement characters found).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-7413`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-airworthiness-release` or\n  `:approve-return-to-service` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops`: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  aviation-safety guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the\n  *capability* to finalize an airworthiness-clearance/return-to-\n  service decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a\n  human gate. For a decision that can directly cost lives in flight\n  with no in-flight correction possible, the capability itself must\n  not exist in the allowlist at all -- this is a stricter bar than the\n  merely-escalated de-energization/lockout-clearance authorization\n  precedent in `cloud-itonami-isco-7413`, reflecting that this batch's\n  hazard framing is the highest-stakes of any Wave3 batch so far.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"engine\" \"repair\"\n  \"airworthiness\"]`): rejected per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class -- this actor's entire domain is aircraft-engine maintenance,\n  so bare nouns would self-trip on essentially every legitimate\n  proposal (work-record logs naming \"inspect turbine blade wear on\n  engine 2\", crew-schedule proposals naming \"borescope inspection\",\n  safety flags naming \"engine-defect\" concerns, supply orders naming\n  \"turbine blade set and borescope consumables\", etc.).\n- Checking aircraft/mechanic provenance only from the proposal (never\n  the request): rejected -- the aircraft-mismatch check requires the\n  aircraft identity to be anchored at the request level and\n  independently cross-checked against anything the proposal claims, so\n  a proposal that quietly names a different, unverified aircraft is\n  caught even if the proposal otherwise looks well-formed.\n- Modeling the mechanic-registration check as unconditionally required\n  on every op: rejected as too rigid -- `:coordinate-supply-order`\n  legitimately has no mechanic to reference (it is a materials-\n  procurement proposal against the aircraft, not a person); the check\n  applies only when a proposal actually references a `:mechanic-id`.\n- Folding the airworthiness-clearance/return-to-service hazard into\n  the same generic \"execution\" phrase family used by the\n  electrical-line-crew reference (no airworthiness-specific phrases):\n  rejected -- an airworthiness-clearance or return-to-service\n  determination is materially distinct from a generic \"perform the\n  repair directly\" phrase (a proposal could name an airworthiness\n  release or return-to-service sign-off specifically without using\n  generic execution language), so dedicated phrases (\"declare the\n  engine airworthy and cleared for return to service\", \"finalize the\n  airworthiness clearance\", \"finalize the return-to-service\n  determination\", \"sign off the airworthiness release\", \"issue the\n  airworthiness release\") and dedicated regression tests\n  (`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-declare-airworthy-return-to-service`,\n  `hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-airworthiness-clearance`)\n  were added independently of the generic maintenance-execution\n  phrases.\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 20000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 27, :assertions 69, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Aircraft Engine Maintenance-Bay Scheduling & Logistics Coordination Service"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :aircraft-verification :mechanic-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-order-cost-threshold-escalation :always-escalate-safety-concern :airworthiness-clearance-scope-exclusion :return-to-service-determination-scope-exclusion :maintenance-execution-finalization-scope-exclusion :certified-aviation-inspector-override-scope-exclusion :certified-mechanic-override-scope-exclusion)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7232"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7232"), (:adr/scope (vector "cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7232" "kotoba-lang/occupation")), (:test/simulator :aerocoord.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7232 — Aircraft Engine Mechanics and Repairers Maintenance-Bay Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7232)))
